1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,649
Crush a sugar crystal into powder.

2
00:00:02,649 --> 00:00:04,257
Then finer, and finer.

3
00:00:04,257 --> 00:00:05,828
Can you go on forever?

4
00:00:06,228 --> 00:00:10,041
Water from a well and water from
the rain have the same recipe.

5
00:00:10,041 --> 00:00:13,052
And when a candle burns,
where does the wax go?

6
00:00:13,502 --> 00:00:17,276
Diya wonders about this in her
kitchen, grinding sugar for tea.

7
00:00:17,626 --> 00:00:22,425
Over two hundred years ago, one scientist
answered all three with one idea.

8
00:00:22,925 --> 00:00:26,813
By the end, you will know which parts
of it survived, and which broke.

9
00:00:27,463 --> 00:00:28,975
So what was the idea?

10
00:00:29,325 --> 00:00:33,541
In eighteen oh eight, John Dalton
published a new system of chemistry.

11
00:00:33,541 --> 00:00:35,257
At its heart is one word.

12
00:00:35,257 --> 00:00:36,157
Atom.

13
00:00:36,123 --> 00:00:37,985
Keep cutting a grain of matter.

14
00:00:37,985 --> 00:00:40,139
Two pieces, four, eight, sixteen.

15
00:00:40,489 --> 00:00:43,924
At the end, you reach a piece
that cannot be cut again.

16
00:00:43,924 --> 00:00:45,542
Dalton called it an atom.

17
00:00:45,942 --> 00:00:49,151
An atom is the smallest
particle of an element.

18
00:00:49,151 --> 00:00:52,044
Dalton pictured it as a hard, tiny ball.

19
00:00:52,694 --> 00:00:56,303
Let's put Dalton's four
ideas on our molecule bench.

20
00:00:56,653 --> 00:00:57,553
Idea one.

21
00:00:57,530 --> 00:01:01,496
All matter is made of tiny
atoms that cannot be divided.

22
00:01:01,496 --> 00:01:04,125
Indivisible means it cannot be split.

23
00:01:04,525 --> 00:01:05,425
Idea two.

24
00:01:05,398 --> 00:01:09,228
All atoms of one element
are identical, even in mass.

25
00:01:09,228 --> 00:01:12,601
Atoms of different elements
have different masses.

26
00:01:13,001 --> 00:01:13,901
Watch.

27
00:01:13,667 --> 00:01:18,166
One oxygen atom on the left pan,
hydrogen atoms on the right.

28
00:01:18,166 --> 00:01:22,032
It takes about sixteen
hydrogens to balance one oxygen.

29
00:01:22,532 --> 00:01:23,557
Idea three.

30
00:01:23,557 --> 00:01:28,537
Compounds form when atoms of different
elements join in a fixed ratio.

31
00:01:28,937 --> 00:01:33,922
In water, oxygen shares a pair of
electrons with each of two hydrogens.

32
00:01:33,922 --> 00:01:36,436
Always two hydrogens to one oxygen.

33
00:01:36,936 --> 00:01:37,942
Idea four.

34
00:01:37,942 --> 00:01:41,073
A chemical reaction only rearranges atoms.

35
00:01:41,073 --> 00:01:44,529
No atom is made, and no atom is destroyed.

36
00:01:44,929 --> 00:01:48,400
Hydrogen and oxygen
molecules break their bonds.

37
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:51,645
The atoms change partners, and form water.

38
00:01:52,045 --> 00:01:53,151
Count them.

39
00:01:53,151 --> 00:01:56,168
Four hydrogens and two oxygens before.

40
00:01:56,168 --> 00:01:59,125
Four hydrogens and two oxygens after.

41
00:01:59,725 --> 00:02:04,374
Now compare water with hydrogen
peroxide, a mild antiseptic.

42
00:02:04,374 --> 00:02:07,194
Both hold only hydrogen and oxygen.

43
00:02:07,594 --> 00:02:10,806
Water has two hydrogens for each oxygen.

44
00:02:10,806 --> 00:02:14,751
Hydrogen peroxide has one
hydrogen for each oxygen.

45
00:02:15,151 --> 00:02:16,336
Pause and think.

46
00:02:16,336 --> 00:02:17,617
Same two elements.

47
00:02:17,617 --> 00:02:19,446
Are they the same compound?

48
00:02:22,646 --> 00:02:23,546
No!

49
00:02:23,113 --> 00:02:26,700
The ratio is different, so
the compound is different.

50
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:29,406
Each compound keeps its own fixed ratio.

51
00:02:30,056 --> 00:02:33,825
So how does this explain the
laws of chemical combination?

52
00:02:33,825 --> 00:02:34,910
Link by link.

53
00:02:35,260 --> 00:02:36,160
Link one.

54
00:02:36,110 --> 00:02:38,506
Atoms are never made or destroyed.

55
00:02:38,506 --> 00:02:40,911
So the total mass stays the same.

56
00:02:40,911 --> 00:02:43,742
That is the law of conservation of mass.

57
00:02:44,492 --> 00:02:45,392
Link two.

58
00:02:45,345 --> 00:02:50,171
Each atom has a fixed mass, and each
compound a fixed ratio of atoms.

59
00:02:50,171 --> 00:02:52,210
So the mass ratio is fixed.

60
00:02:52,210 --> 00:02:54,987
That is the law of definite proportions.

61
00:02:55,737 --> 00:02:56,805
Link three.

62
00:02:56,805 --> 00:03:02,003
Two grams of hydrogen join sixteen
grams of oxygen in water, but thirty

63
00:03:02,003 --> 00:03:04,440
two grams in hydrogen peroxide.

64
00:03:05,190 --> 00:03:09,749
Sixteen to thirty two is one to
two, a simple whole number ratio.

65
00:03:09,749 --> 00:03:12,528
That is the law of multiple proportions.

66
00:03:13,278 --> 00:03:14,389
Remember this.

67
00:03:14,389 --> 00:03:17,579
In every reaction, atoms
only change partners.

68
00:03:18,279 --> 00:03:20,014
Now, one worked example.

69
00:03:20,314 --> 00:03:25,110
Eighteen grams of water always holds
two grams of hydrogen and sixteen

70
00:03:25,110 --> 00:03:26,492
grams of oxygen.

71
00:03:26,992 --> 00:03:31,505
How much hydrogen and oxygen are
in seventy two grams of water?

72
00:03:32,005 --> 00:03:32,905
Step one.

73
00:03:32,887 --> 00:03:35,757
Seventy two divided by eighteen is four.

74
00:03:35,757 --> 00:03:38,477
So we have four times as much water.

75
00:03:39,227 --> 00:03:40,164
Step two.

76
00:03:40,164 --> 00:03:43,328
Hydrogen is four times two, eight grams.

77
00:03:43,328 --> 00:03:46,960
Oxygen is four times
sixteen, sixty four grams.

78
00:03:47,710 --> 00:03:48,610
Check.

79
00:03:48,311 --> 00:03:50,796
Eight plus sixty four is seventy two.

80
00:03:50,796 --> 00:03:52,708
Nothing made, nothing lost.

81
00:03:53,358 --> 00:03:55,380
But not every idea survived.

82
00:03:55,780 --> 00:03:57,066
Idea one broke.

83
00:03:57,066 --> 00:04:00,966
Atoms do have parts, electrons,
protons and neutrons.

84
00:04:00,966 --> 00:04:03,155
We meet them in chapter two.

85
00:04:03,555 --> 00:04:04,996
Idea two broke too.

86
00:04:04,996 --> 00:04:08,078
Atoms of one element can
have different masses.

87
00:04:08,078 --> 00:04:12,690
These are isotopes, like chlorine
thirty five and chlorine thirty seven.

88
00:04:13,090 --> 00:04:16,855
Ideas three and four still hold
for every chemical reaction.

89
00:04:17,255 --> 00:04:18,554
And it had gaps.

90
00:04:18,554 --> 00:04:23,079
It could not explain the law of
gaseous volumes, or say why atoms join

91
00:04:23,079 --> 00:04:23,979
at all.

92
00:04:24,454 --> 00:04:25,920
Now, the exam trap.

93
00:04:26,270 --> 00:04:30,468
Students write that all atoms of an
element have the same mass, because

94
00:04:30,468 --> 00:04:31,574
Dalton said so.

95
00:04:31,974 --> 00:04:33,508
But isotopes break it.

96
00:04:33,508 --> 00:04:37,125
Chlorine atoms come with mass
thirty five or thirty seven.

97
00:04:37,125 --> 00:04:39,093
Same element, different mass.

98
00:04:39,743 --> 00:04:41,225
Where is this asked?

99
00:04:41,225 --> 00:04:42,796
As statement questions.

100
00:04:42,796 --> 00:04:46,415
Which postulate still holds,
and which law it explains.

101
00:04:47,015 --> 00:04:48,281
Exam level now.

102
00:04:48,281 --> 00:04:53,227
Which statement of Dalton's atomic
theory is still accepted without change?

103
00:04:53,227 --> 00:04:54,546
Pause and solve.

104
00:04:59,946 --> 00:05:01,212
The answer is C.

105
00:05:01,712 --> 00:05:02,612
A fails.

106
00:05:02,611 --> 00:05:05,950
Atoms have electrons,
protons and neutrons.

107
00:05:06,450 --> 00:05:07,350
B fails.

108
00:05:07,284 --> 00:05:11,560
Isotopes of chlorine have masses
thirty five and thirty seven.

109
00:05:12,060 --> 00:05:12,960
D fails.

110
00:05:12,953 --> 00:05:16,041
Dalton could not explain gaseous volumes.

111
00:05:16,041 --> 00:05:19,905
Only C survives: a reaction
just rearranges atoms.

112
00:05:20,505 --> 00:05:21,968
Here is your recap.

113
00:05:21,968 --> 00:05:23,471
Screenshot this card.

114
00:05:26,671 --> 00:05:29,507
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

115
00:05:29,907 --> 00:05:30,807
One.

116
00:05:30,452 --> 00:05:33,529
State Dalton's four
ideas in your own words.

117
00:05:34,329 --> 00:05:35,229
Two.

118
00:05:34,905 --> 00:05:39,271
How does idea four explain the
law of conservation of mass?

119
00:05:40,071 --> 00:05:40,971
Three.

120
00:05:40,742 --> 00:05:43,732
Which of Dalton's ideas do isotopes break?

121
00:05:44,632 --> 00:05:49,899
Next time: an atom is too tiny to
weigh, so how do we know oxygen is about

122
00:05:49,899 --> 00:05:52,389
sixteen times heavier than hydrogen?

123
00:05:52,389 --> 00:05:55,663
We find out in atomic
mass and molecular mass.
